8	// This file extends the strict-text rule (see stricttext.go for the JSON
9	// entry) to a release configuration constructed directly in Go memory and
10	// handed to ValidateReleaseInput or MakeReleasePlan.
11	//
12	// A Go string can hold bytes that are not valid UTF-8. Without this check
13	// such bytes would survive into planning unchanged at first, but two
14	// distinct corrupt identities would be indistinguishable the moment the
15	// plan is written as JSON — encoding/json rewrites every invalid rune to
16	// the replacement character "�" (U+FFFD) — and corrupt tag keys or values
17	// would silently take part in label matching and fault-domain batching. The
18	// check therefore runs as the first stage of ValidateReleaseInput, before
19	// any business rule and hence before selection or batching: the config is
20	// rejected outright rather than producing a plan that looks successful.
21	//
22	// Legal text is used exactly as written — including Chinese, supplementary
23	// plane characters such as "😀", and a literally written "�" — and ordinary
24	// text that merely contains a backslash followed by "uD800" is not invalid
25	// UTF-8 (only the JSON entry deals with \uXXXX escapes at all).
26	//
27	// When several strings are invalid, exactly one deterministic problem is
28	// reported. Scalars are checked in their fixed field order; candidates and
29	// conditions at their real zero-based positions (disabled clusters and
30	// clusters that filtering would drop are checked just the same); within one
31	// tags object or condition the entries are examined in ascending key order,
32	// so map iteration order can never change the error. A value error names
33	// the key it belongs to; an invalid key names the tags object or condition
34	// object owning it.
35	
36	// invalidConfigTextError reports a string in a directly constructed config
37	// that contains bytes which are not valid UTF-8. where is a field-and-
38	// position description in the same style as the business-validation errors:
39	// a top-level field for scalars, clusters[i] (plus the offending tag key or
40	// the owning tags object) for candidate data, and include[i]/exclude[i]
41	// (plus the condition key or owning condition object) for conditions.
42	type invalidConfigTextError struct {
43		where string
44	}
45	
46	func (e *invalidConfigTextError) Error() string {
47		return fmt.Sprintf("内存配置包含无效 UTF-8 字节: %s", e.where)
48	}
